PROMPT_CASE_RUN explain status=Completed error=None result=Some("Read `src/limit.rs` (3 lines). Here's what it does:\n\n**What `first_lines` actually returns**\n\n```rust\npub const MAX_LINES: usize = 3;\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    (0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n\n- Despite the name and `MAX_LINES = 3`, it returns **4 elements**, not 3: `0..=MAX_LINES` is an *inclusive* range over indices `0, 1, 2, 3`. That's an off-by-one — the likely intent was `0..MAX_LINES` or `lines[..MAX_LINES]`.\n- It returns an owned `Vec<String>` with each selected line cloned from the `&str` slice, preserving order: `lines[0]`, `lines[1]`, `lines[2]`, `lines[3]`.\n- There is no validation, truncation, or defaulting — it's direct indexing.\n\n**What happens for short inputs**\n\n- If `lines.len() < 4`, the function **panics** with an out-of-bounds index error, because `lines[i]` indexes unchecked:\n  - Empty slice → panics on the very first iteration: `index out of bounds: the len is 0 but the index is 0`.\n  - Length 1 → panics at `i = 1`; length 2 → panics at `i = 2`; length 3 → panics at `i = 3`.\n- So it does **not** gracefully return fewer lines for short input; a 3-line input, which one would expect to be fine given `MAX_LINES = 3`, still panics.\n\n**Safe fix if you want it later** (not applied): `lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()` — returns up to 3 lines and handles any length without panicking.\n\nNo files were changed.")
