PROMPT_CASE_RUN review status=Completed error=None result=Some("Verified against the source. The file matches the reviewer's evidence, and the finding is correct.\n\n## Review result: src/limit.rs — 1 confirmed defect\n\n**HIGH — Inclusive range causes guaranteed panic at the documented limit and off-by-one truncation**\n\n`src/limit.rs:3`:\n```rust\n(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n```\nWith `MAX_LINES = 3` (`src/limit.rs:1`), `0..=3` yields 4 indices (0, 1, 2, 3):\n\n- **Panic:** Inputs of length ≤ 3 — including the intended \"exactly 3 lines\" case — index `lines[3]`, panicking with `index out of bounds`. `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])` and even `first_lines(&[])` abort the caller; there is no bounds check or error path.\n- **Off-by-one:** Inputs with ≥ 4 elements return 4 lines instead of the 3 the constant and function name promise.\n\n**Smallest fix** (panic-free for all inputs, including empty):\n```rust\nlines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()\n```\n\n**Clean by absence:** no rate-limiting/window/reset logic, arithmetic, casts, division, time handling, or shared state exists in this file, so those correctness dimensions have no exposure.\n\n**Coverage limits:** `src/` contains only `limit.rs`; there is no `lib.rs`/`main.rs`, test, or caller, so I could not adjudicate from usage whether \"3\" or \"4\" is the intended cardinality — the panic is a defect under either interpretation. Diagnostics were unavailable (rust-analyzer health warning), so this is a source-level review; the panic is confirmable by inspection of line 3. No files were changed.")
