src/limit.rs:3`\n```rust\n(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n```\n\n**Why it's wrong (concrete failing scenarios):**\n\n- **Off-by-one:** With `MAX_LINES = 3`, the range `0..=3` produces 4 indices. Any input with ≥4 elements returns 4 lines, not the 3 the constant and function name promise. `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"])` returns `[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]`.\n- **Panic (index out of bounds):** The primary intended input — exactly `MAX_LINES` lines — panics: `first_lines(&[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"])` panics at `lines[3]` with `index out of bounds: the len is 3 but the index is 3`. Shorter inputs, including `first_lines(&[])`, panic the same way. Since the slice is indexed directly with no bounds check or error path, every input of length ≤ `MAX_LINES` aborts the caller rather than returning the truncated result.\n\n**Smallest fix (handles both the count and the panic, including the empty case):**\n```rust\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n(If a range must be kept for style: `lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES)...` is still preferable to `0..MAX_LINES` + `min(lines.len())`, since `take` cannot panic on any input.)\n\n## Coverage notes\n\n- No other files exist in the workspace: `src/` contains only `limit.rs`, and there is no `lib.rs`, `main.rs`, test, or config referencing `first_lines`/`MAX_LINES` (grep across the workspace matched only the definitions themselves). So there is no caller evidence to adjudicate whether \"3 lines\" or \"4 lines\" is the intended cardinality — but the panic scenario is a defect under either interpretation.\n- No rate-limiting, reset/window/decay logic, integer casts, arithmetic, division, time handling, or shared mutable state exists in this file, so those dimensions are clean by absence.\n- `src/limit.rs` is not dead code by declaration (`pub`), but with no module root it is currently unreferenced; if that's unexpected, the 